description:
Stacked on #802 (base branch `claude/elegant-tesla-mja103-ptype`), which is stacked on #801, #800, #796 and #795. #831 sits on top of this one and declares the DBItest version.

`dbConnect(arrow = TRUE, lazy_strings = TRUE)` opts into leaving the character columns of fetched data frames as nanoarrow's views into Arrow arrays instead of copying the strings out. No ALTREP class of RSQLite's own: the views are nanoarrow's, and the cost they carry today is to be taken upstream. Default `FALSE`.

**Conversion.** This layer changes only fetches with a lazy column. Every other fetch, including every fetch without `lazy_strings`, keeps the conversion of the layers below unchanged: one array is converted at once, and several arrays go through the R-level copy loop of #796, which releases each array as soon as it is copied. Converting eager fetches column by column as well took as long and peaked higher, because `nanoarrow::convert_array_stream()` keeps its arrays until the next garbage collection; the experiment README records that comparison. A fetch with a lazy column and at least one array is split by column in C++ (`arrow_split_columns()`), which moves the children of its struct arrays into one stream per column, so that a view holds the strings of its own column alone. `arrow_lazy_columns()` then replaces only the conversion step of `arrow_to_df()`. The lazy columns are concatenated, and nanoarrow fills each of the other columns in one vector with the prototype as its target. Factors, times in seconds, the prototypes' attributes, the narrowing of integers, `bigint` and the coercion warning, raised after the conversion, follow as for every other fetch.

**`lazy_strings`.** A character column without a prototype, or with a plain `character()` one, is concatenated into one array in C++ (`arrow_concat_strings()`). It hands a single array on as it is, frees each chunk once copied, and builds `large_utf8` when the strings need more than the 2^31 - 1 bytes that `utf8` offsets address. Factors, blobs and any column with another prototype stay eager, and factors without levels are built from copies. All arrays of a lazy fetch count towards the young-generation collection that `dbFetchArrowChunk()` already triggers every 64 MB, since the collector does not see their memory. The option requires `arrow = TRUE`, is validated and cloned like `arrow`, and is documented in `?SQLite` and in the "Data frames" section of `?"sqlite-arrow"`, including its price: most vectorised uses of a view convert its strings again, one by one, so a column used more than once is better copied with `c()` first.

**Tests.** `tests/testthat/test-DBItest-arrow.R` and the new `test-DBItest-arrow-lazy.R` share a helper, `helper-DBItest-arrow.R`, that builds the context and runs the groups that fetch or write data frames: `test_driver()`, which checks `bigint` with `dbGetQuery()`, plus `test_result()`, `test_sql()`, `test_meta()`, `test_transaction()` and `test_arrow()`. The getting started, connection, compliance and stress groups call nothing on this path. With DBItest 1.8.3, the eager and the lazy contexts each pass 8033 expectations with the same 59 skipped tests. This layer keeps `dbitest_version = "1.8.1"` as the layers below do, and #831 declares the newer version. `tests/testthat/test-arrow-lazy.R` covers:

- validation and cloning of the option;
- equality with the eager data frames across full fetches, chunks, empty tails and zero rows;
- views that outlive the result, the connection and a garbage collection;
- prototypes of every kind, converted from one array and from one array per row;
- times in seconds and the coercion warning;
- the collection trigger;
- `large_utf8` with a lowered limit;
- with `RSQLITE_TEST_LARGE_ARROW=true` (about 2.5 GB of memory, with chunks cut at 64 MiB and garbage collected before and after), a text column of 2049 MiB.

**Benchmark.** `experiments/2026-09-26-arrow-df-fetch/` gains a run on `fbd54c9`, whose R and C++ code is that of this head. The harness now reads the peak RSS before `object.size()`, which had added up to 170 MB to the cells with text. Five million rows, medians of two repetitions, on an Ubuntu 24.04 container with 4 cores of an Intel Xeon at 2.1 GHz, R 4.5.3, SQLite 3.54.1 and nanoarrow 0.9.0. Timings move by about 10 % between repetitions on this shared machine, and the peak RSS repeats to the MB.

- All columns, full fetch:
  - lazy strings: 3.6 s and 642 MB above the floor;
  - eager: 9.4 s and 730 MB, which is the conversion of the layers below;
  - default path: 15.7 s and 614 MB.
- The strings alone: 2.0 s lazy, 7.5 s eager and 11.6 s on the default path.
- A loop of `dbFetch(n = 10000)` that discards its data frames: 3.3 s and 159 MB lazy, 6.8 s and 133 MB eager.
- Numbers-only cells do not move.
- Small fetches cost more, measured separately on 100,000 rows of five columns, three repetitions. Per fetch of 10 rows: 0.26 to 0.36 ms lazy, 0.17 to 0.21 ms eager, 0.06 ms on the default path. Per fetch of 100 rows: 0.29 to 0.37, 0.19 to 0.25 and 0.12 to 0.16 ms. From 1,000 rows on, the three are within noise.

## Review

A clean-context review (2026-09-27) found one High issue (lazy strings beyond 2 GiB per column failed the fetch), one Medium (the driver and transaction specs were missing from the Arrow DBItest runs) and one Low (dead code). All are fixed after merging the final versions of the layers below. Its suggestion to declare a newer `dbitest_version` is taken up in #831. The integration review of the whole stack at 071560ec found no issue in this layer's code. It compared lazy and eager data frames on 3,000 random tables, with and without `ptype` and with two-row chunks, ran the tests under AddressSanitizer, and ran `gctorture()` around the split and the concatenation. Its note that this description had fallen behind the code is addressed here.

What remains is listed under [Accepted limitations](https://github.com/r-dbi/RSQLite/pull/807#issuecomment-6778104404).

The test fixtures this pull request adds live in `tests/testthat/helper-arrow.R`, so every `test_that()` block runs on its own right after `load_all()`. `is_lazy()` is named `is_lazy_strings()` there.

